---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ukgaap/frsse.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope – What Qualified as a \"Smaller Entity\"" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Withdrawal – Superseded by FRS 102 Section 1A and FRS 105" },
        { id: "s4", label: "What the FRSSE Historically Provided" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Old UK GAAP Context – the Pre-2015 Standards Landscape" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Worked Example – Historical FRSSE Simplifications" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Current Treatment – Section 1A and FRS 105" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Disclosure Requirements (Current, Post-Withdrawal)" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Critical Judgments" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Relationship to IAS/IFRS – Then and Now" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">The pre-2015 simplified regime for small companies</div><div class="principle">Before the WHOLESALE 2015 overhaul of UK GAAP, the FRSSE (Financial Reporting Standard for Smaller Entities) provided a SELF-CONTAINED, SIMPLIFIED set of accounting requirements for small companies – allowing them to avoid applying the FULL suite of "old UK GAAP" standards (the individual SSAPs, old FRSs, and UITF Abstracts that existed BEFORE FRS 100–105 replaced them). With the introduction of "new UK GAAP," the FRSSE's ROLE was ABSORBED into FRS 102's OWN Section 1A small-entity relief, and – for the very smallest companies – into the NEW FRS 105 micro-entities regime, making the FRSSE REDUNDANT and leading to its WITHDRAWAL.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope – What Qualified as a "Smaller Entity"</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Companies meeting the Companies Act "small company" size thresholds</div><div class="principle">The FRSSE HISTORICALLY applied to companies (and certain other entities) meeting the UK/Irish Companies Act's "SMALL COMPANY" size thresholds of the time – broadly similar in SPIRIT to today's small-entity thresholds, though the SPECIFIC monetary limits have been updated multiple times since the FRSSE's withdrawal.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Withdrawal – Superseded by FRS 102 Section 1A and FRS 105</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A deliberate consolidation, not a rejection of simplification itself</div><div class="principle">The FRC WITHDREW the FRSSE effective for accounting periods beginning on or after <strong>1 JANUARY 2016</strong>, as part of the BROADER "new UK GAAP" project – the FRSSE's PURPOSE (proportionate simplicity for small companies) was NOT abandoned, but rather RE-HOMED within the NEW framework: ORDINARY small companies now use FRS 102's <strong>SECTION 1A</strong> reduced disclosure regime, while the VERY SMALLEST companies (micro-entities) can use the NEW, EVEN SIMPLER <strong>FRS 105</strong> standard instead.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>What the FRSSE Historically Provided</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A single, self-contained document covering recognition, measurement, and disclosure</div><div class="principle">The FRSSE was DELIBERATELY designed as a SINGLE, SELF-CONTAINED standard – bringing together SIMPLIFIED recognition, measurement, and disclosure requirements covering the FULL range of topics a small company might encounter, WITHOUT requiring reference to the FULL, more extensive suite of "old UK GAAP" individual standards that larger companies were required to apply.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Old UK GAAP Context – the Pre-2015 Standards Landscape</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely different regulatory era, predating today's IFRS-aligned suite</div><div class="principle">BEFORE 2015, "old UK GAAP" consisted of a PATCHWORK of individually issued Statements of Standard Accounting Practice (SSAPs, dating from the 1970s-80s), later Financial Reporting Standards (old FRSs, issued from the 1990s onward), and Urgent Issues Task Force (UITF) Abstracts – a genuinely DIFFERENT, less internationally-aligned body of standards than TODAY's FRS 100–105 suite, which is EXPLICITLY based on IFRS (and the IFRS for SMEs Standard specifically for FRS 102). The FRSSE existed as the SIMPLIFIED alternative WITHIN that OLDER landscape.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example – Historical FRSSE Simplifications</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">How a small company's accounts used to look</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A small trading company's pre-2016 accounts</div><div class="wk-body">A qualifying small UK company prepared its accounts under the FRSSE for a financial year beginning before 1 January 2016.</div><div class="wk-answer">The company applied the FRSSE's SELF-CONTAINED requirements – a SINGLE document covering all the topics it needed, WITHOUT needing to separately consult the full range of "old UK GAAP" SSAPs and FRSs. Following the 2015 overhaul, the SAME company, for periods beginning ON or AFTER 1 January 2016, would instead apply <strong>FRS 102 Section 1A</strong> (or FRS 105, if it also meets the NEWER micro-entity thresholds) – a genuinely DIFFERENT, though similarly PROPORTIONATE, simplified framework.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Current Treatment – Section 1A and FRS 105</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Two successor regimes, layered by entity size</div><div class="principle">TODAY, a small company applies FRS 102 in FULL, but with SECTION 1A's reduced DISCLOSURE requirements (NOT reduced recognition/measurement) if it meets the Companies Act small company thresholds; if it ALSO meets the STRICTER micro-entity thresholds, it may instead elect the FURTHER-simplified FRS 105 (see the companion posters) – TOGETHER replacing the single FRSSE document with a MORE granular, TWO-TIER simplification structure within the "new UK GAAP" hierarchy.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements (Current, Post-Withdrawal)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What applies today instead</div><div class="check-list"><span>FRS 102 (full recognition/measurement) with Section 1A reduced disclosures, for qualifying small companies</span><span>FRS 105 (simplified recognition/measurement AND disclosure), for qualifying micro-entities</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Critical Judgments</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most (historically and today)</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">HISTORICAL JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a company met the "smaller entity" size thresholds in force at the time</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">CURRENT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a company meets the CURRENT small-company or micro-entity thresholds, and which successor regime (Section 1A or FRS 105) to elect</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS – Then and Now</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A pre-IFRS-alignment standard, now replaced by an explicitly IFRS-anchored suite</div><div class="principle">The FRSSE, as part of "OLD UK GAAP," was developed LARGELY INDEPENDENTLY of IFRS, reflecting UK-specific accounting tradition and Companies Act requirements of its era, WITHOUT the deliberate international alignment that characterises TODAY's suite. Its SUCCESSOR regimes – FRS 102 Section 1A and FRS 105 – by contrast, sit WITHIN a framework EXPLICITLY based on (and adapted from) the IASB's own IFRS for SMEs Standard – representing a GENUINE philosophical shift from the FRSSE era's more INSULAR UK-specific approach toward today's DELIBERATELY IFRS-anchored hierarchy (see the FRS 100 and FRS 102 posters for the fuller picture of this alignment).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where FRSSE-related presentation used to land</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected (historically)</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Small company simplified accounts</span><span>BS / P&amp;L (Companies Act formats)</span><span>WITHDRAWN – superseded by FRS 102 Section 1A / FRS 105</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>FRS 102 (Section 1A)</span><span>Successor framework – supplies the reduced disclosure regime now applicable to qualifying small companies</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>FRS 105</span><span>Successor framework for the smallest qualifying (micro-entity) companies</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">FRSSE (historical term)</div><div class="glossary-def">The Financial Reporting Standard for Smaller Entities – a self-contained simplified standard for small companies, withdrawn for periods beginning on or after 1 January 2016.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Old UK GAAP</div><div class="glossary-def">The pre-2015 body of UK accounting standards (SSAPs, old FRSs, UITF Abstracts) that the FRSSE operated alongside, since replaced by the FRS 100–105 suite.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.frsse || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
